---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import FAQSchema from "../components/FAQSchema.astro";
import RelatedGuides from "../components/RelatedGuides.astro";
import { platformById } from "../data/platforms";

const polymarketEligibility = platformById["polymarket"]!.eligibility.usStatus;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Are prediction markets legal?",
    a: `It depends where you live. In the US, Kalshi operates under CFTC regulation. Polymarket's US availability: ${polymarketEligibility} Other platforms vary by country. Always check your local rules and the platform's terms before signing up.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Is a prediction market the same as gambling?",
    a: "They share similarities, but prediction markets are built around real-world events and their prices reflect the crowd's estimated probability of an outcome. Regulated markets like Kalshi are treated as financial event contracts, not casino games.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do prices work?",
    a: "Contracts typically trade between 0 and 100 cents (or $0-$1). A price of 65 cents means the market estimates roughly a 65% chance of that outcome. If you're right, each contract settles at $1; if not, it settles at $0.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much money do I need to start?",
    a: "Most platforms let you start with a small amount: often $1 to $20. Never deposit more than you can comfortably afford to lose.",
  },
  {
    q: "What happens when a market resolves?",
    a: "When the event outcome is confirmed, winning contracts settle at $1.00 each and losing contracts settle at $0. Your profit or loss is automatically credited to your account balance.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I exit a trade before the event resolves?",
    a: "Yes. You can sell your contracts at any time while the market is open. If the probability has moved in your favor, you can take a profit early. If your view has changed, you can cut losses before settlement.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="What is a prediction market?"
  description="A prediction market is a marketplace where you trade contracts on the outcome of real-world events. Here's how they work, whether they're legal, and how to start."
  path="/what-is-a-prediction-market"
>
  <article class="prose wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The basics</p>
    <h1>What is a prediction market?</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      A prediction market is a marketplace where people buy and sell contracts on
      the outcome of a real-world event: like an election, a game, or an economic
      figure. The price of each contract reflects the crowd's estimate of how
      likely that outcome is.
    </p>

    <h2>How they work, in plain English</h2>
    <p>
      Each market asks a yes/no question: for example, "Will inflation be above 3%
      this year?" You can buy "Yes" or "No" contracts. Prices move between 0 and
      100 cents as people trade. A "Yes" price of 65 cents roughly means the market
      thinks there's a 65% chance the answer is yes.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the event resolves, the winning side's contracts are worth $1 each and
      the losing side's are worth $0. Your profit is the difference between what you
      paid and what it settled at.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Quick example.</strong> You buy 10 "Yes" contracts at 40 cents ($4 total).
      The event happens, so each settles at $1: you receive $10, a $6 profit. If it
      hadn't happened, you'd have lost the $4.
    </div>

    <h2>Why prices are interesting</h2>
    <p>
      Because real money is on the line, prediction-market prices often track
      reality surprisingly well: they aggregate what many informed people believe.
      That's why journalists and researchers increasingly cite them as a real-time
      probability gauge.
    </p>

    <h2>Are they legal? Who can use them?</h2>
    <p>
      This is the part that trips people up, so be precise about your own location:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>United States:</strong> Kalshi operates under CFTC regulation. Polymarket is <em>not</em> available to US persons.</li>
      <li><strong>Outside the US:</strong> Availability varies. Polymarket and Crypto.com serve many regions but not all.</li>
      <li><strong>Age:</strong> You generally must be 18+ (sometimes 21+). Check each platform.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>See our <a href="/compare">side-by-side comparison</a> to find what's available where you are.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <FAQSchema faqs={faqs} />

    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:2.5rem;">
      <a class="btn" href="/compare">Compare the platforms →</a>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--ink-soft);">
      Educational content only, not financial advice. Trade responsibly and only
      with money you can afford to lose.
      <a href="/risks-and-responsible-trading">Read our full risks page →</a>
    </p>
    <RelatedGuides slug="what-is-a-prediction-market-beginners-guide" />
  </article>
</Base>
